   },
   {
    "i": 4,
    "t0": 11.14,
    "t1": 14.74,
    "path": "out/narration/line_04.wav",
    "text": "If the replay lost, its start can't have been quite the same.",
    "phrases": [
     [
      11.14,
      12.45
     ],
     [
      12.75,
      14.74
     ]
    ]
   }
  ],
  "need": 15.94
 },
 "twins": {
  "lines": [
   {
    "i": 5,
    "t0": 0.4,
    "t1": 6.409,
    "path": "out/narration/line_05.wav",
    "text": "Here are two chips, released a thousandth of a millimetre apart. They fall together, then split.",
    "phrases": [
     [
      0.4,
      3.74
     ],
     [
      4.14,
      5.18
     ],
     [
      5.48,
      6.409
     ]
    ]
   },
   {
    "i": 6,
    "t0": 7.4,
    "t1": 10.76,
    "path": "out/narration/line_06.wav",
    "text": "One wins ten thousand dollars. Its twin wins a hundred.",
    "phrases": [
     [
      7.4,
      8.97
     ],
     [
      9.37,
      10.76
     ]
    ]
   },
   {
    "i": 7,
    "t0": 11.56,
    "t1": 15.501,
    "path": "out/narration/line_07.wav",
    "text": "A difference far too small to see, and a completely different result.",
    "phrases": [
     [
      11.56,
      13.351
     ],
     [
      13.651,
      15.501
     ]
    ]
   }
  ],
  "need": 16.7
 },
 "chaos": {
  "lines": [
   {
    "i": 8,
    "t0": 0.6,
    "t1": 9.8,
    "path": "out/narration/line_08.wav",
    "text": "This is the heart of chaos theory. It studies systems that follow exact rules, with no randomness at all, yet still can't be predicted for long.",
    "phrases": [
     [
      0.6,
      2.54
     ],
     [
      3.04,
      7.62
     ],
     [
      7.92,
      9.8
     ]
    ]
   },
   {
    "i": 9,
    "t0": 10.6,
    "t1": 30.85,
    "path": "out/narration/line_09.wav",
    "text": "It began with the weather. In 1961, the meteorologist Edward Lorenz reran a simulation, typing in point five zero six, instead of point five zero six one two seven. A difference of about one part in four thousand. Two months into the simulated weather, the two forecasts had nothing in common.",
    "phrases": [
     [
      10.6,
      11.74
     ],
     [
      12.14,
      17.4
     ],
     [
      17.6,
      22.53
     ],
     [
      22.93,
      26.17
     ],
     [
      26.57,
      30.85
     ]
    ]
   },
   {
    "i": 10,
    "t0": 31.65,
    "t1": 40.984,
    "path": "out/narration/line_10.wav",
    "text": "Here are two runs of Lorenz's equations, starting almost exactly together. For a while, they move as one. Then they part, and never agree again.",
    "phrases": [
     [
      31.65,
      36.04
     ],
     [
      36.44,
      38.205
     ],
     [
      38.705,
      39.454
     ],
     [
      39.754,
      40.984
     ]
    ]
   },
   {
    "i": 11,
    "t0": 41.784,
    "t1": 48.214,
    "path": "out/narration/line_11.wav",
    "text": "Chaos theory calls this sensitive dependence on initial conditions. Lorenz called it the butterfly effect.",
    "phrases": [
     [
      41.784,
      45.724
     ],
     [
      46.124,
      48.214
     ]
    ]
   },
   {
    "i": 12,
    "t0": 48.914,
    "t1": 51.054,
    "path": "out/narration/line_12.wav",
    "text": "A Plinko board is a perfect example.",
    "phrases": [
     [
      48.914,
      51.054
     ]
    ]
   }
  ],
  "need": 52.25
 },
 "mechanism": {
  "lines": [
   {
    "i": 13,
    "t0": 0.5,
    "t1": 3.743,
    "path": "out/narration/line_13.wav",
    "text": "So why is Plinko chaotic? Because the pins are round.",
    "phrases": [
     [
      0.5,
      2.023
     ],
     [
      2.423,
      3.743
     ]
    ]
   },
   {
    "i": 14,
    "t0": 4.543,
    "t1": 11.895,
    "path": "out/narration/line_14.wav",
    "text": "Bounce two chips off a flat wall, a hair apart, and they leave a hair apart. A flat wall faces the same way everywhere.",
    "phrases": [
     [
      4.543,
      7.465
     ],
     [
      7.765,
      9.365
     ],
     [
      9.665,
      11.895
     ]
    ]
   },
   {
    "i": 15,
    "t0": 12.695,
    "t1": 24.875,
    "path": "out/narration/line_15.wav",
    "text": "A round pin is different. Its surface faces a slightly different way at every point. So two chips that hit it a hair apart meet slopes tilted at different angles, and bounce off in different directions.",
    "phrases": [
     [
      12.695,
      14.105
     ],
     [
      14.405,
      17.275
     ],
     [
      17.675,
      22.215
     ],
     [
      22.515,
      24.875
     ]
    ]
   },
   {
    "i": 16,
    "t0": 25.475,
    "t1": 30.125,
    "path": "out/narration/line_16.wav",
    "text": "The further they travel, the further apart they get. Then the next pin does it again.",
    "phrases": [
     [
      25.475,
      28.295
     ],
     [
      28.595,
      30.125
     ]
    ]
   },
   {
    "i": 17,
    "t0": 30.925,
    "t1": 40.181,
    "path": "out/narration/line_17.wav",
    "text": "In the simulator, each bounce multiplies the gap by about two point two. Over a whole drop, about thirty-six bounces, that's nearly a trillion times.",
    "phrases": [
     [
      30.925,
      35.015
     ],
     [
      35.415,
      38.161
     ],
     [
      38.361,
      40.181
     ]
    ]
   },
   {
    "i": 18,
    "t0": 40.881,
    "t1": 45.744,
    "path": "out/narration/line_18.wav",
    "text": "Doubling, over and over: that's exponential growth, the engine of chaos.",
    "phrases": [
     [
      40.881,
      42.234
     ],
     [
      42.534,
      44.084
     ],
     [
      44.384,
      45.744
     ]
    ]
   }
  ],
  "need": 46.94
 },
 "horizon": {
  "lines": [
   {
    "i": 19,
    "t0": 0.5,
    "t1": 10.015,
    "path": "out/narration/line_19.wav",
    "text": "So how far ahead can you see? Know the start to a millimetre, and you can predict about three bounces. To the width of a single atom, just twenty-four.",
    "phrases": [
     [
      0.5,
      2.35
     ],
     [
      2.85,
      6.92
     ],
     [
      7.32,
      8.915
     ],
     [
      9.115,
      10.015
     ]
    ]
   },
   {
    "i": 20,
    "t0": 10.715,
    "t1": 15.196,
    "path": "out/narration/line_20.wav",
    "text": "To call a whole drop, you'd need the start to within the size of an atomic nucleus.",
    "phrases": [
     [
      10.715,
      11.836
     ],
     [
      12.036,
      15.196
     ]
    ]
   }
  ],
  "need": 16.4
 },
 "crux": {
  "lines": [
   {
    "i": 21,
    "t0": 0.5,
    "t1": 9.51,
    "path": "out/narration/line_21.wav",
    "text": "Here's the real test. Find a drop that wins, from anywhere across the top, then replay it from exactly the same spot, as precisely as you like.",
    "phrases": [
     [
      0.5,
      1.63
     ],
     [
      2.13,
      4.96
     ],
     [
      5.26,
      9.51
     ]
    ]
   },
   {
    "i": 22,
    "t0": 10.31,
    "t1": 21.76,
    "path": "out/narration/line_22.wav",
    "text": "Match it to a micron, and it wins again just sixteen percent of the time, barely more than a fresh drop. Match it to a single atom: thirty-nine percent. Still worse than a coin flip.",
    "phrases": [
     [
      10.31,
      14.3
     ],
     [
      14.6,
      16.18
     ],
     [
      16.68,
      18.34
     ],
     [
      18.64,
      19.86
     ],
     [
      20.26,
      21.76
     ]
    ]
   },
   {
    "i": 23,
    "t0": 22.56,
    "t1": 26.27,
    "path": "out/narration/line_23.wav",
    "text": "More precision always helps. But certainty never arrives.",
    "phrases": [
     [
      22.56,
      24.0
     ],
     [
      24.6,
      26.27
     ]
    ]
   }
  ],
  "need": 27.47
 },
 "islands": {
  "lines": [
   {
    "i": 24,
    "t0": 0.5,
    "t1": 9.696,
    "path": "out/narration/line_24.wav",
    "text": "On the show, you can let go anywhere across the top, at the pins or up to a foot above. Each dot here is one of those spots. The gold ones win.",
    "phrases": [
     [
      0.5,
      3.286
     ],
